@anon.9bf146a73cb71d9c7e3fe69f46c42f4a.818 = private unnamed_addr constant <{ [1099 x i8] }> <{ [1099 x i8] c"Where to [align] something along an axis.\0A\0APossible values are:\0A- `start`: Aligns at the [start]($direction.start) of the [text\0A  direction]($text.dir).\0A- `end`: Aligns at the [end]($direction.end) of the [text\0A  direction]($text.dir).\0A- `left`: Align at the left.\0A- `center`: Aligns in the middle, horizontally.\0A- `right`: Aligns at the right.\0A- `top`: Aligns at the top.\0A- `horizon`: Aligns in the middle, vertically.\0A- `bottom`: Align at the bottom.\0A\0AThese values are available globally and also in the alignment type's scope,\0Aso you can write either of the following two:\0A\0A```example\0A#align(center)[Hi]\0A#align(alignment.center)[Hi]\0A```\0A\0A# 2D alignments\0ATo align along both axes at the same time, add the two alignments using the\0A`+` operator. For example, `top + right` aligns the content to the top right\0Acorner.\0A\0A```example\0A#set page(height: 3cm)\0A#align(center + bottom)[Hi]\0A```\0A\0A# Fields\0AThe `x` and `y` fields hold the alignment's horizontal and vertical\0Acomponents, respectively (as yet another `alignment`). They may be `{none}`.\0A\0A```example\0A#(top + right).x \\\0A#left.x \\\0A#left.y (none)\0A```" }>, align 1
